    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      "@id": "https://github.com/Navin009/auto-permission-system#faq",
      "mainEntity": [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is auto-permission-system?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A Pi package (npm:auto-permission-system) that adds a zero-trust permission system and a Claude-style tool view to the Pi coding agent."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the Pi permission system?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A policy engine enforced in two layers plus a redactor: an OS sandbox around bash (bubblewrap on Linux, sandbox-exec on macOS), an in-process tool guard for read, grep, find, ls, write, edit, fetch_content, web_search and MCP tools, and a scan that hides credentials in tool output before the model reads it."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is Claude auto mode for Pi?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "An autonomous but fail-closed posture for unattended Pi runs: pi -p, CI jobs, containers and subagents keep working with no human at the keyboard, and anything unapproved is blocked with a reason instead of hanging. Setting `filesystem.outsideProject.read` to `deny` and `network.strictAllowlist` to `true` enables it."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the Pi tool view?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A display-only renderer: /tool-view compact draws each run of shell calls, reads and codemode as one Claude-style row with a mark, a count and a single timer, while /tool-view full (the default) leaves Pi's own output untouched. It never reads or writes the sandbox policy."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is auto secure mode?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "The advanced-secure permission mode: it keeps every policy rule and adds secret and credential detection, MCP risk scoring and real-time redaction, and can only add asks or blocks, never loosen one. Choose it with /permission-mode."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does it work on Windows?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Layers 2 and 3 are platform independent, so the tool guard and the secret redactor run on Windows. Layer 1 (the OS sandbox for bash) requires bubblewrap and socat on Linux or the built-in sandbox-exec on macOS."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is there a security extension for the Pi coding agent?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes. auto-permission-system is a security extension for the Pi coding agent, installed with pi install npm:auto-permission-system. It adds an OS sandbox around bash (bubblewrap on Linux, sandbox-exec on macOS), an in-process tool guard over the built-in tools and MCP calls, secret redaction before tool output reaches the model, and a JSONL audit log of every decision. It is a Pi extension, not a Claude Code plugin, and requires no account, API key or hosted service."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the Pi agent permission system?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "The Pi permission system (also written permission system for Pi or permissions for Pi) is one plain-JSON sandbox.json enforced by two layers: the OS sandbox around bash and the in-process tool guard, with the Auto Secure redactor on top, ask-tier prompts for reads, writes, domains and MCP calls, a project-trust step, and an audit log. Every rule is readable and editable, and no allow or deny is decided by the model."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Why was my tool call or command blocked?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Run /security to see the effective policy and the last 10 audit events, then check ~/.pi/agent/audit.log for the matching rule and its reason. A name-only result (ask-read-name-only) never asks, and a refused path stays refused for the rest of the conversation (ask-read-remembered-deny) instead of prompting again."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How much does auto-permission-system cost?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Nothing. It is MIT licensed, runs locally, and adds no account, API key or telemetry."
          }
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
